WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.437
Sometimes numerical data arrives in separate blocks and needs to be combined.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:09.377
In this example, January and February each contain three values.

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:17.032
N P dot vstack places one array above the other, creating a two-dimensional array with one row for

00:00:17.032 --> 00:00:19.047
January and one row for February.

00:00:19.047 --> 00:00:22.347
Click Run and look at the vertical stack.

00:00:22.347 --> 00:00:30.172
Then N P dot concatenate joins the two one-dimensional arrays end to end, creating one longer sequence.

00:00:30.172 --> 00:00:36.192
These operations produce different shapes, even though they use the same source values.

00:00:36.192 --> 00:00:38.572
That is why shape matters.

00:00:38.572 --> 00:00:43.127
Before combining arrays, ask what the new dimensions should mean.

00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:47.427
If rows represent months, vertical stacking is sensible.

00:00:47.427 --> 00:00:53.162
If you simply want one continuous sequence, concatenation may be more appropriate.

00:00:53.162 --> 00:00:58.962
NumPy also provides horizontal stacking and more general concatenate operations for combining arrays along

00:00:58.962 --> 00:01:01.598
different axes.
